## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a simple, predictable memory allocator suitable for tight RTOS constraints, with deterministic O(1) operations and minimal fragmentation.

## Key Concepts

- Fixed-size pool design
- Free-list management via indices
- Interrupt-safe critical sections
- Pointer arithmetic and alignment
- Stress-testing under ISR vs task context

## Code Example

```c
#define BLOCK_SIZE 64
#define BLOCK_COUNT 512
static uint8_t pool_mem[BLOCK_SIZE*BLOCK_COUNT];
static uint16_t next_index[BLOCK_COUNT];
static uint16_t free_head;

void pool_init(void) {
  for (uint16_t i = 0; i < BLOCK_COUNT-1; ++i) next_index[i] = i+1;
  next_index[BLOCK_COUNT-1] = 0xFFFF;
  free_head = 0;
}

void* pool_alloc(void) {
  if (free_head == 0xFFFF) return NULL;
  uint16_t idx = free_head;
  free_head = next_index[idx];
  return pool_mem + idx * BLOCK_SIZE;
}

void pool_free(void* ptr) {
  uintptr_t off = (uintptr_t)ptr - (uintptr_t)pool_mem;
  uint16_t idx = off / BLOCK_SIZE;
  next_index[idx] = free_head;
  free_head = idx;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to multiple block sizes?
- How would you detect and recover from pool exhaustion in real-time constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests fundamental low-level synchronization and memory ordering concepts in a realistic micro-optimization context.

## Key Concepts

- Atomic flags and test_and_set semantics
- memory_order_acquire vs memory_order_release
- Busy-wait with cpu pause for reduced contention
- Backoff strategies to avoid cache-line thrashing

## Code Example

```javascript
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef atomic_flag spinlock_t;
void spinlock_init(spinlock_t* s) { atomic_flag_clear(s); }
void spinlock_lock(spinlock_t* s) { while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(s, memory_order_acquire)) { _mm_pause(); } }
void spinlock_unlock(spinlock_t* s) { atomic_flag_clear_explicit(s, memory_order_release); }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you modify to minimize starvation?
- How would you test for fairness across threads under high contention?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of basic synchronization primitives, correctness under contention, and memory ordering pitfalls in a beginner-friendly yet non-trivial way.

## Key Concepts
- Atomic operations and memory orders
- FIFO fairness and blocking behavior
- Spinlock design trade-offs and wrap-around handling
- Basic micro-benchmarking considerations under contention

## Code Example
```javascript
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <stdint.h>

typedef struct {
  atomic_uint64_t next_ticket;
  atomic_uint64_t now_serving;
} ticket_lock_t;

void ticket_lock_acquire(ticket_lock_t *lock) {
  uint64_t my = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lock->next_ticket, 1, memory_order_relaxed);
  while (atomic_load_explicit(&lock->now_serving, memory_order_acquire) != my) {
    // busy-wait
  }
}

void ticket_lock_release(ticket_lock_t *lock) {
  atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&lock->now_serving, 1, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for fairness under CPU migrations on a NUMA system?
- What are practical mitigations to reduce bus traffic on high contention?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess understanding of low-level synchronization, atomic operations, and memory ordering in a practical, portable way.

## Key Concepts

- Spinlock using atomic_flag
- Memory ordering: acquire on lock, release on unlock
- Exponential backoff with PAUSE to reduce contention
- Correctness test with concurrent increments

## Code Example

```c
#include <stdatomic.h>
#include <immintrin.h>

typedef struct { atomic_flag flag; } spinlock;

static inline void spinlock_init(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear(&s->flag);
}

static inline void spin_lock(spinlock* s) {
  while (atomic_flag_test_and_set_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_acquire)) {
    _mm_pause();
    // small exponential backoff
    for (int b = 1; b < 1024; b <<= 1) {
      _mm_pause();
    }
  }
}

static inline void spin_unlock(spinlock* s) {
  atomic_flag_clear_explicit(&s->flag, memory_order_release);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this for NUMA systems to reduce cross-socket traffic?
- What are the trade-offs of using a ticket-lock vs this spinlock in high-contention workloads?
